Ray Dillinger wrote:
...
> What's the usual setup for making a tournament server work, anyway?  
> 
> In the simple case, I invoke nethack (or any roguelike game using 
> the console) from the command line in a console session via Telnet or 
> SSH. 
> 
> But on a tournament server, it should just be login with username and
> password, and you're in the game program.  You don't want to use a 
> ..login file that invokes the game under a shell, because a well-timed
> control-c would then land a user at a command line from which s/he 
> might do, um, naughty things. Can a .login script trap control-c?
> 
> Do you just use the game program itself as the login shell?  If so 
> does the game need to do anything (beyond being playable over telnet) 
> to support this use? I guess avoiding 'suspend' or 'shell' commands 
> that rely on a shell would be necessary.  And trapping control-c etc.  
> 
> Do you use a chroot directory to lock the game environment down 
> securely?  
...

We use a custom login shell written in Perl; this lets us do things like 
detect already-running game sessions and try to kill them gracefully 
before starting a new one.

We do not currently trap ctrl-c, but it's on the feature request list 
from last year in a big way.  ;-)

The game server kit for this year should be available for download in 
about a week (several game servers each year are provided by volunteers 
who offer to host and administer game servers), at which point you'll be 
able to look through the new game server side at your leasure; the new 
Challenge for this year is requiring a huge extension of the kit, and 
I'm not done with that yet.

(The web site will automatically open downloads on the currently 
available game server kit on October 1st, but that will probably still 
be last year's kit; we have to move into a new colo facility on 
September 30th, so I may not get the code done by then.)

Most of the Tournament logic is in the control server kit, which we've 
never released.  This really isn't to keep it secret; it's mostly just 
been that it would be a lot of work to separate it from the specific 
server environment and to make sure we'd removed the ssh keys, database 
passwords and such from the scripts.

We really should do chroot, but given the wide variety of systems on 
which game servers are usually hosted it can get really hairy to set 
that up (and we've often had volunteers with systems that don't _have_ 
chroot available).  We do recommend that all game servers be freshly 
installed before the Tournament and wiped clean afterwards.

-robin


